From: Yang Tsao <yang@fydeos.io>
Subject: [PATCH] Widen the tablet multitask menu hit region for non-browser windows

In tablet mode the multitask menu ("snap window panel") is opened by
dragging down from the top of a window, and on FydeOS that drag almost
never lands: the target is 200x10 dip, pinned to the window's top edge.
Ten dip is about 1.6mm of glass — these are DIP, so the display's scale
factor buys pixels, not finger room — and the only visual affordance for
it is the 48x4 dip cue at y=6, so what you can see is smaller still and
sits in the bottom four dip of what you must hit.

It was not always this small. Upstream crrev.com/c/7065652 cut it from
200x100 for one specific reason:

    In ChromeOS tablet mode, with WebUI tabstrip disabled (soon to be the
    default), the multitask menu in the top center of a window makes it
    hard to initiate drag of a tab located in that area.

That conflict is real, and only a browser window has it — the tabs live
in exactly that strip. Every other window pays for a clash it cannot
have. ARC windows feel it worst, because they are frameless in tablet
mode: with no caption there is no other affordance, so a window that has
gone fullscreen has the menu as its only way back, and a target this
small makes that a trap.

So choose the height per window rather than globally: browser windows
keep upstream's 10 dip and its tabstrip fix is untouched; everything
else — ARC, Chrome apps, Crostini, system — gets the pre-crrev.com/c/7065652
100 dip back.

AppType::BROWSER is the discriminator rather than "does this window have
a tabstrip", which is deliberately conservative: a browser window with
the WebUI tabstrip enabled has no top tabs to clash with either and
could take the taller region too, but asking that question from ash
means reaching into browser state for a case that costs nothing to skip.

---
 .../tablet_mode_multitask_menu_controller.cc          | 19 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git src.orig/ash/wm/tablet_mode/tablet_mode_multitask_menu_controller.cc src/ash/wm/tablet_mode/tablet_mode_multitask_menu_controller.cc
index d20b458cc35ea..5fdb823b2dc6f 100644
--- src.orig/ash/wm/tablet_mode/tablet_mode_multitask_menu_controller.cc
+++ src/ash/wm/tablet_mode/tablet_mode_multitask_menu_controller.cc
@@ -14,6 +14,8 @@
 #include "ash/wm/window_state.h"
 #include "ash/wm/window_util.h"
 #include "base/functional/bind.h"
+#include "chromeos/ui/base/app_types.h"
+#include "chromeos/ui/base/window_properties.h"
 #include "ui/aura/client/capture_client.h"
 #include "ui/aura/env.h"
 #include "ui/events/event.h"
@@ -31,6 +33,18 @@ namespace {
 // window/tab dragging.
 constexpr gfx::SizeF kHitRegionSize(200.f, 10.f);
 
+// FydeOS: the same region for a window that has no tabstrip to clash with.
+//
+// The 10dip height above came from crrev.com/c/7065652, whose problem was
+// specific: "with WebUI tabstrip disabled, the multitask menu in the top
+// center of a window makes it hard to initiate drag of a tab located in that
+// area". Only a browser window has tabs there. Every other window pays the
+// cost — a 10dip target is about 1.6mm of glass whatever the display's scale
+// factor, since these are DIP — for a conflict it cannot have, and on ARC
+// windows in particular the panel is close to undraggable. Restore the
+// pre-crrev.com/c/7065652 height for those.
+constexpr gfx::SizeF kHitRegionSizeNoTabstrip(200.f, 100.f);
+
 // Returns true if `window` can show the menu and `screen_location` is in the
 // menu hit bounds.
 bool HitTestRect(aura::Window* window, const gfx::PointF& screen_location) {
@@ -39,7 +53,10 @@ bool HitTestRect(aura::Window* window, const gfx::PointF& screen_location) {
   }
   const gfx::RectF window_bounds(window->GetBoundsInScreen());
   gfx::RectF hit_region(window_bounds);
-  hit_region.ClampToCenteredSize(kHitRegionSize);
+  hit_region.ClampToCenteredSize(
+      window->GetProperty(chromeos::kAppTypeKey) == chromeos::AppType::BROWSER
+          ? kHitRegionSize
+          : kHitRegionSizeNoTabstrip);
   hit_region.set_y(window_bounds.y());
   return hit_region.Contains(screen_location);
 }
